  "summary": "New preprints link COPS5, meiotic stress pathways, HLH-30/TFEB and rRNA-dependent histone processing to germline function, alongside Drosophila RNA-binding and snoRNP studies.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how specific regulators sustain gene expression programs and genome integrity in germline and gametogenic contexts. In mouse germ cells, COPS5 loss downregulates piRNA pathway genes, lowers pachytene piRNAs, derepresses LINE1 and IAP, and arrests spermatogenesis. In budding yeast meiosis, Hsf1, Skn7, Msn2 and Msn4 are active without external stress, and TSA1 and AHP1 protect gametes. In C. elegans, HLH-30/TFEB loss alters the basal transcriptome and reduces sperm and progeny numbers, while disrupted rRNA biogenesis causes histone mRNA 3' misprocessing. Drosophila work adds Marf1 integration into accessory gland regulation and GAR1-linked H/ACA snoRNP control of sgg splicing and insulin signaling. Ovarian aging and mitochondrial-nuclear yeast screens extend the scope beyond germline silencing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
